Keeler’s (Futurama) Theorem
Suppose a permutation π ∈ S_n is written as a product of distinct transpositions.
Keeler’s Theorem states that it is always possible to undo π using only distinct transpositions, provided we enlarge the domain by two new elements.

Theorem
Let 
    π ∈ S_n
be a permutation expressible as a product of distinct transpositions.
Then there exists a permutation
    σ ∈ S_{n+2}
such that
σπ = id.


Proof (Keeler)
Let π be a k-cycle on [n], written as
π= 1 2 ... k k+1 ... n
   2 3 ... 1 k+1 ... n 

Extend  π to a permutation π* on [n] ∪ {x,y}
by fixing the two new elements x and y

Define σ as the left‑to‑right composition of the following distinct transpositions:
σ = (x1)(y2)(y3)...(yk)(x2)(y1)

Each transposition swaps one of the new elements x or y with an element of [n], ensuring that no transposition is repeated.
A direct computation shows that
π* σ = 1 2 ... n x y
       1 2 ... n y x
Thus σ restores every element of [n] to its original position and swaps only 
x and y.
If desired, the final transposition (xy) returns them to their original positions as well.
Since any permutation decomposes into disjoint cycles, applying this construction to each cycle independently yields a product of distinct transpositions that inverts the entire permutation.
□
